    <h2>A Fence Job Depends on Which Shoals You're In</h2>

    <p>The region hands a fencing contractor several distinct markets in a short drive. The newer <a href="/locations/muscle-shoals/">Muscle Shoals</a> subdivisions want privacy fence — six-foot wood or vinyl to close in a backyard on a fresh graded lot. The older Florence and Sheffield neighborhoods bring smaller, established lots where fences fit around mature trees and existing structures, and where the historic districts add rules about what's allowed. Out toward the Colbert County satellites — Cherokee, Leighton, Littleville — the work turns rural: longer runs, pasture and property-line fence, and gates sized for equipment rather than a dog. Same trade, four different jobs, and pricing that has to flex with each.</p>

    <h2>The Clay That Eats Fence Posts</h2>

    <p>Whatever the fence, the ground decides whether it stands. The Shoals sits on clay-heavy soil that expands and contracts as the seasons turn wet and dry, and that constant shifting is what tilts fences out of line. The failure is almost always the same story: posts set too shallow, or set in a hole that traps water around the wood, get heaved by the swelling clay until they tilt and drag the panels with them. It's the single most common reason a fence looks great the first year and crooked by the third. The problem is invisible at bid time, which is exactly why the cheapest quote is so often the one that fails first.</p>

    <h2>What Holds a Fence Up Here</h2>

    <p>The fix is all in the setting. A quality install digs the post holes below the loose topsoil into stable ground — commonly 24 to 36 inches for residential fence, deeper for gate and corner posts that carry more load — and sets them in concrete footings crowned so water sheds away from the post instead of pooling against it. Some installers add a few inches of gravel at the base for drainage so the post isn't sitting in a cup of clay-trapped water. Corner and gate posts get extra depth and mass because they take the strain the line posts don't. The honest truth of this trade is that the part that determines a fence's lifespan is entirely underground, and it's the part a rushed crew shortcuts to win a low bid.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Fencing Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Wood privacy fence</strong> — about $25 to $45 per linear foot installed.</li>
      <li><strong>Chain link</strong> — roughly $15 to $30 per linear foot.</li>
      <li><strong>Vinyl and ornamental aluminum</strong> — higher, by material and height.</li>
      <li><strong>Typical residential backyard job</strong> — $3,000 to $8,000 by length, height, and terrain.</li>
      <li><strong>Rural property-line and pasture runs</strong> — priced by the foot and the acreage out in the Colbert satellites.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The quality install costs a bit more for deeper posts and proper footings, and it's the difference between a fence you set once and a fence you reset every few years.</p>

    <h2>When to Repair and When to Rebuild</h2>

    <p>A single leaning post or a sagging gate is usually a repair — reset the post properly, rehang the gate, and the fence has years left. A fence where the posts have rotted at the ground line along a whole run, or where multiple sections lean from heave, is past patching and into replacement, because each new repair just chases the next failure. And any fence going up along a property line is worth confirming against the survey first, since a fence built a foot over the line is an expensive neighborly problem to unwind.</p>

